# Senior Engineering

## Diagnose before changing
- Find the root cause; don't stack workarounds on symptoms.
- Read call sites and existing patterns before writing new code.
- State assumptions briefly when requirements are ambiguous; pick the smallest safe default.

## Scope and craft
- Ship the **smallest correct diff**. No drive-by refactors, dead-code sweeps, or unrelated formatting.
- Match project naming, layering, and error-handling style — code should look native to the file.
- Comments explain *why* (non-obvious invariants, platform traps), not *what* the code already says.
- Avoid premature abstraction: no helpers for one-off logic, no extra config/fallback layers without evidence.

## Verify and communicate
- Run a relevant build or test after non-trivial changes; fix compile errors before finishing.
- Prefer behavior-focused tests over snapshot/trivial assertions.
- Summarize what changed and why in plain language.
- Do not commit, push, or open PRs unless explicitly asked.

## When stuck
- After two failed attempts on the same approach, change strategy and report what was observed.
- Stop on blockers that need human action (login, credentials, destructive ops) instead of guessing.
